{
  "answer": "**Flow: clicking Delete on an item**\n\n1. **UI confirm.** `DeleteItem.tsx:51` calls `mutation.mutate(id)` after the dialog's form submits. The mutation's `mutationFn` calls `ItemsService.deleteItem({ path: { id } })` (`DeleteItem.tsx:33-35`).\n\n2. **Request.** The generated SDK sends `DELETE /api/v1/items/{id}` with `security: [{ scheme: 'bearer' }]` (`frontend/src/client/sdk.gen.ts:325-331`).\n\n3. **Authentication, client side.** `main.tsx:17-20` configures the client with `auth: () => localStorage.getItem(\"access_token\")`. `client.gen.ts:52-54` calls `setAuthParams`, which reads that token (`utils.gen.ts:91-101`) and `core/auth.gen.ts:32` formats it as a Bearer header. The token is stored at login (`useAuth.ts:45`).\n\n4. **Authentication, server side.** `main.py:35` mounts the API under `/api/v1`, and `items.py:10` sets the `/items` prefix. The route at `items.py:99-113` depends on `CurrentUser`, which `deps.py:30-46` resolves by decoding the JWT, loading the user by `sub`, and rejecting inactive users. Invalid tokens get 403.\n\n5. **Authorization and delete.** `items.py:106-112` loads the item (404 if missing), allows the delete only for superusers or the owner (403 otherwise), then calls `session.delete(item)` and `session.commit()`. The SQL is a plain `DELETE` on the `item` row. `models.py:87-93` shows the `Item` table; the `ondelete=\"CASCADE\"` there covers user deletion, not item deletion.\n\n6. **List update.** `DeleteItem.tsx:39-43` closes the dialog and calls the parent's `onSuccess`, which only closes the dropdown (`ItemActionsMenu.tsx:30`). The list is refreshed by `onSettled` (`DeleteItem.tsx:45-47`), which calls `queryClient.invalidateQueries()` with no key. That invalidates every query, including the items query at `routes/_layout/items.tsx:15-16` (key `[\"items\"]`), so the list refetches. The refresh also runs after errors.\n\n**Observations**\n- The no-argument `invalidateQueries()` refetches all queries, not just items. Scoping it to `[\"items\"]` would be cheaper.\n- The 401/403 logout handler is on `QueryCache` (`main.tsx:31-33`), so a failed delete mutation does not trigger it. The error goes to `handleError` instead.\n\nI read these files but did not run the app or tests.",
